'''Gunma Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|群馬県}}, ''Gunma-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located on the western coast of the island of [[Ishijima]], facing the [[Katsuran Sea]]. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 1,790,531, with approximately 38% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Gunma, Gunma|Gunma]] (pop. 733,554). Gunma Prefecture occupies the western interior of Ishijima, positioned between the heavy manufacturing zone of [[Aizawa Prefecture]] to the north and the volcanic highlands of the [[Asuka Range]] to the south. The prefecture is the precision engineering heartland of the Ishijima industrial belt, producing machine tools, industrial robotics, and semiconductor fabrication equipment that supply both the domestic keiretsu and international markets.

Gunma's relationship with the Katsuran Sea—rather than the open Shendan Ocean—has shaped a fundamentally different economic and cultural trajectory from its eastern neighbour. Where Ishijima Prefecture faces the vast, typhoon-lashed expanse of the Shendan, Gunma looks west across the sheltered Katsuran Sea toward [[Harujima]] and the metropolitan core of [[Okami]]. This western orientation has made Gunma a bridge between the Ishijima industrial belt and the capital region, a role reinforced by the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] corridor that threads through the prefecture's mountainous interior.

== History ==

=== Ancient and classical period ===
The Katsuran coastal zone of western Ishijima has been inhabited since the second millennium BCE, initially by [[Atsu]] communities who established fishing settlements along the sheltered bays of the Katsuran shoreline. Unlike the exposed Shendan coast, the Katsuran-facing communities developed in relative calm—protected from the open ocean by the bulk of the Ishijima landmass and the island of Harujima beyond the strait.

The area that would become Gunma Prefecture was historically known as the "Inner Gate" ({{lang|ja|内門}}, ''Naimon'') of Ishijima, because the Katsuran Strait provided the shortest crossing between the island and the political centre of power on Harujima. The Naimon communities served as intermediaries between the Harujima court and the Ishijima interior, facilitating the flow of goods, officials, and military forces across the strait. This intermediary role created a distinctive cultural identity—more cosmopolitan than the isolated Shendan coast, yet more Ishijima-oriented than the Aizawa port communities that looked outward to the ocean.

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Katsuran corridor ===
The [[Honda Shogunate]]'s "Maritime Exclusion" ({{lang|ja|海禁}}, ''Kaikin'') policy had a markedly different impact on the Katsuran coast than on the Shendan-facing communities. While the Kaikin severed the western Aizawa coast's trade with the Ogasawara Islands, the Katsuran Sea remained a vital internal waterway connecting Ishijima to Harujima. The Shogunate maintained regular ferry traffic across the Katsuran Strait for administrative purposes, and the Naimon communities continued to serve as the primary conduit for inter-island communication.

The Shogunate period saw the emergence of Gunma's precision metalworking tradition. The Katsuran coastal communities, with their access to iron ore deposits in the Asuka foothills and their position on the Shogunate's internal supply chain, developed a reputation for fine metalwork—particularly swords, tools, and precision instruments. The Gunma Metalworkers' Guild ({{lang|ja|群馬金工組合}}, ''Gunma Kinkō Kumiai''), established in 1687, became one of the most respected craft guilds on Ishijima, producing ceremonial swords for the Shogunate and precision tools for the Aizawa shipyards.

=== The Saito Restoration and industrialization ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 transformed Gunma from a craft-based economy into a precision manufacturing centre. The Shogunate's metalworking tradition provided a skilled workforce that was rapidly adapted to modern industrial production, and the [[Saito Restoration|Saito]] government's "Rich Country, Strong Army" policy directed investment toward the Katsuran corridor as a strategic manufacturing zone—close enough to the capital for quality oversight, yet far enough inland to be protected from naval attack.

The construction of the first Ishijima railway in 1894 linked Gunma to the Aizawa port complex, enabling the rapid shipment of manufactured goods to the western shipping terminals. The Gunma Precision Works, founded by [[Iijima Kōhei]] ({{lang|ja|飯島光平}}) in 1898, became the first factory in Okaiken to produce machine tools to international standards, and its products were exported to the broader Fosian market within a decade.

=== The Restructuring Era and the robotics pivot ===
The [[1990s Restructuring Era]] ({{lang|ja|再構築時代}}, ''Saikōchiku Jidai'') was the defining period for modern Gunma. As the government aggressively intervened to pivot the economy from declining light manufacturing to high-technology and heavy industrial automation, Gunma's precision engineering tradition positioned it perfectly for the transition. The prefecture attracted the bulk of the nation's industrial robotics investment, and the [[Gunma Robotics District]]—a cluster of machine tool manufacturers, industrial robotics firms, and semiconductor equipment producers in the southern suburbs of the capital—became the most concentrated precision engineering zone in Okaiken.

The establishment of the [[Gunma Robotics Institute]] in 1996, affiliated with the [[Aizawa Institute of Technology]], provided the research foundation for the robotics pivot. The institute's work on adaptive manufacturing systems and AI-guided quality control became the technical backbone of the [[Satori AI]] integration that would transform the Ishijima industrial belt in the 2010s.

=== Modern era ===
The [[Satori AI]] integration of the 2010s brought fully automated manufacturing systems to the Gunma Robotics District, with AI-guided machine tools producing precision components to tolerances measured in nanometres. The [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 2026 hit the Gunma district particularly hard—automated production lines went offline for 72 hours, creating a backlog of precision components that disrupted supply chains across the Ishijima industrial belt and the Yagikoshu data corridor. The crisis intensified debates about the developmental liberal state's dependence on centralized AI infrastructure and the vulnerability of just-in-time manufacturing systems.

== Geography ==

Gunma Prefecture occupies the western interior of Ishijima, stretching from the Katsuran Sea coastline in the west to the volcanic highlands of the [[Asuka Range]] in the east. The prefecture is bounded by the [[Katsuran Sea]] to the west, [[Aizawa Prefecture]] to the north, [[Ishijima Prefecture]] to the east, and [[Tochigi Prefecture]] to the south. The [[Asuka Range]]—Okaiken's highest mountain chain—forms the prefecture's eastern border, with [[Mount Asuka]] (4,224 m) shared between Gunma, Ishijima, and Tochigi prefectures.

The Katsuran coastal zone is narrower and more rugged than the broad Shendan plain to the east, but the sheltered waters of the Katsuran Sea provide natural harbours that have served as maritime gateways for centuries. The Gunma River, fed by the glacial meltwater of the Asuka Range, flows westward through the prefectural capital to the Katsuran Sea, providing both freshwater and hydroelectric power.

The interior highlands, rising from the coastal plain toward the Asuka massif, contain significant mineral deposits—particularly iron ore, tungsten, and rare-earth elements—that historically supported the prefecture's metalworking tradition and now supply the precision engineering district. The Gunma Highlands are also home to extensive onsen (hot spring) networks, fed by the geothermal activity of the Asuka volcanic system.

