== Economy ==

Gunma Prefecture's economy is driven by three sectors: precision engineering and robotics, semiconductor equipment manufacturing, and mineral extraction. The prefecture's position as the precision engineering heartland of the Ishijima industrial belt makes it one of the most productive manufacturing regions in Okaiken per capita, despite having a smaller population than the neighbouring Aizawa industrial zone.

=== Precision engineering and robotics ===
The [[Gunma Robotics District]]—a cluster of machine tool manufacturers, industrial robotics firms, and semiconductor equipment producers in the southern suburbs of the capital—is the most concentrated precision engineering zone in Okaiken. Okaiken possesses more than half of Adonia's industrial robots as of 2026, and a significant proportion of those robots are manufactured in Gunma. The district employs approximately 42,000 workers directly and supports an additional 120,000 jobs in the supply chain.

The district's anchor firms include [[Gunma Precision Works]] (machine tools), [[Iijima Robotics]] (industrial automation), and [[Katsuran Semiconductor Equipment]] (fabrication plant tooling). These firms produce the machine tools, robotic arms, and semiconductor lithography equipment that supply the [[Kizan Systems]] foundries on Ishijima and Yagikoshu, the [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] shipyards in Aizawa, and the [[Yonezawa Motors]] assembly plants on Harujima.

The [[Gunma Robotics Institute]], affiliated with the [[Aizawa Institute of Technology]], develops next-generation manufacturing automation systems and serves as the primary research centre for adaptive manufacturing in Okaiken. The institute's work on AI-guided quality control systems was instrumental in the development of the [[Satori AI]] manufacturing platform.

=== Semiconductor equipment ===
Gunma is a critical node in Okaiken's semiconductor supply chain. While the actual fabrication plants are located in Aizawa and Yagikoshu, the precision equipment that fabricates the chips—the lithography machines, deposition systems, and etching tools—is manufactured in Gunma. [[Katsuran Semiconductor Equipment]], the district's largest firm, produces approximately 40% of the domestic semiconductor equipment market and exports to fabrication plants across Fosia and beyond.

The semiconductor equipment sector is the prefecture's most valuable export, generating approximately 円120 billion in annual revenue. The sector's extreme precision requirements—features measured in nanometres—have driven continuous innovation in the Gunma Robotics District, creating a virtuous cycle between equipment manufacturing and the broader precision engineering ecosystem.

=== Mineral extraction ===
The Gunma Highlands, rising from the coastal plain toward the Asuka massif, contain significant mineral deposits that have supported the prefecture's metalworking tradition for centuries. The [[Gunma Mining Corporation]], a state-backed enterprise established in 1952, operates three active mines in the highlands producing iron ore, tungsten, and rare-earth elements. The rare-earth output, while modest compared to the deep-sea mining operations in the Ogasawara EEZ, provides a domestic source of critical materials for the precision engineering and semiconductor equipment sectors.

The mining sector has contracted significantly since its peak in the 1970s, when the highlands employed over 15,000 miners. As of 2026, the sector employs approximately 2,800 workers, but the remaining operations are highly automated and produce higher-grade ore than the historical mines. The [[Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation]]'s recycling facility in Yonezawa has reduced demand for virgin rare-earth extraction, though the Gunma mines remain strategically important as a domestic source of supply.

=== Onsen wellness ===
The geothermal activity of the Asuka Range feeds an extensive onsen network in the Gunma highlands, particularly in the communities of Akagi, Yoshida, and the mountain town of Asuka. The [[Shirakawa-Yunoyama]] onsen district, while primarily located in neighbouring [[Nagayama Prefecture]], extends into the Gunma highlands, and the prefecture's own onsen towns attract approximately 800,000 visitors annually.

The [[Cult of Iron]]'s [[Tesshin-yu]] ({{lang|ja|鉄身湯}}, "Iron Body Bath") athletic recovery program has created a significant market for Gunma's mineral-rich hot springs, with several onsen towns deriving an estimated 25% of their revenue from registered athletes. The Akagi Highland Onsen, known for its high iron content, is particularly popular among [[Kenka-Jutsu]] practitioners and [[X-League]] athletes.
Gunma's high-altitude precision machine shops occupy a vital independent niche within the national economy. Operating outside the formal keiretsu cross-shareholding structures, [[Gunma Precision Works]] and affiliated machine-tool guilds manufacture specialized multi-axis CNC tooling, high-pressure titanium valve manifolds, and robotic actuators supplied to all six major industrial conglomerates. In the surrounding mountain valleys, traditional sericulture cooperatives preserve pure-strain silkworm breeding, producing ultra-high-tensile silk filaments utilized for precision optical components, ceremonial robes, and aerospace acoustic insulation.

== Culture ==

=== The metalworking tradition ===
Gunma's cultural identity is inseparable from its metalworking heritage. The [[Gunma Metalworkers' Guild]], established in 1687, maintains a living tradition of precision metalwork that bridges the Shogunate-era craft guilds and the modern robotics industry. The guild's annual exhibition, held in the prefectural capital every October, showcases work ranging from traditional ceremonial swords to nanometre-precision semiconductor tooling—a juxtaposition that the guild presents as a continuous tradition of "making things that make things" ({{lang|ja|物を作る物を作る}}, ''Mono o tsukuru mono o tsukuru'').

The guild's apprentice system, which requires a minimum seven-year training period, has been adapted for the modern era. Apprentices now train in both traditional metalworking and CNC machining, and the guild operates a certification programme for precision engineers that is recognized across the Ishijima industrial belt.

=== Cuisine ===
Gunma's cuisine reflects its inland position and its metalworking heritage. The signature dish is Katsuran Iron Pot Stew ({{lang|ja|桂鉄鍋}}, ''Katsuran Tetsu Nabe''), a hearty stew of mountain vegetables, wild boar, and fermented soybean paste, slow-cooked in a cast-iron pot over charcoal. The dish is traditionally prepared by metalworkers during the winter months and served at guild gatherings—a practice that dates to the Shogunate period.

Other local specialties include Gunma soba ({{lang|ja|群馬蕎麦}}, ''Gunma soba'')—buckwheat noodles made with mountain spring water from the Asuka highlands—and Akagi smoked trout ({{lang|ja|赤城燻製鱒}}, ''Akagi kunsei masu''), cured over smouldering cedar chips from the highland forests.

=== Music ===
Gunma's industrial landscape has produced a distinctive musical subculture. The "machine music" ({{lang|ja|機械音}}, ''Kikai-on'') scene, pioneered by producers in the Gunma Robotics District, samples the rhythmic patterns of CNC machines, robotic assembly lines, and semiconductor lithography equipment to create electronic music that celebrates the precision engineering aesthetic. The genre has gained a devoted following in the Okami underground and is frequently featured at the annual Gunma Machine Music Festival.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] (Red Line) passes through Gunma Station, connecting the prefecture to [[Aizawa Station]] to the northwest and [[Toyozawa Station]] to the south. The Gunma Katsuran Line, a conventional rail service, runs along the western coast connecting the prefectural capital to the Katsuran Sea ports.

=== Air ===
Gunma Airport (GMA) serves as a regional hub for domestic flights, with connections to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]]. The airport's inland position provides more reliable service than the typhoon-exposed Ishijima Airport on the eastern coast.

=== Maritime ===
The [[Port of Gunma]], on the Katsuran Sea, handles approximately 420,000 shipping containers annually. While smaller than the Aizawa and Ishijima ports, the Gunma port serves as a critical logistics hub for the precision engineering sector, receiving raw materials from the Harujima metropolitan region and exporting finished machine tools and semiconductor equipment. Regular ferry services connect Gunma to ports on the western coast of Harujima, with crossing times of approximately four hours to the [[Okami]] metropolitan area.

== Education ==

Gunma Prefecture is home to the [[Gunma Robotics Institute]], the premier research centre for manufacturing automation in Okaiken, and the [[Gunma Institute of Precision Engineering]], which trains the next generation of machine tool engineers and semiconductor equipment designers. The [[Gunma Metalworkers' Guild]] operates a traditional apprenticeship programme that is recognized as a cultural heritage designation by the Okaiken Cultural Preservation Agency.

